      "content": "\\begin{table}[htbp]\\centering\\scriptsize\n\\def\\sym#1{\\ifmmode^{#1}\\else\\(^{#1}\\)\\fi}\n\\caption{\\textbf{Heterogeneity Conditional on Market Risk and Competitive Position}}\n\\label{heterogeneity2}\n\n\\noindent{\\justifying{This table presents the heterogeneous association between local patent trading infrastructure and institutional ownership conditional on corporate market risk and competitive position for a sample of Chinese A-share listed firms from 2003 to 2023. Firms are divided into subsamples based on the annual median values of Financial Distress (measured by \\textsl{Z-score}), Share Turnover (measured by \\textsl{Turnover}), and Market Position (measured by \\textsl{MarketShare}). Specifically,\\textsl{Z-score} proxies for financial risk based on liquidity and solvency; \\textsl{Turnover} proxies for market speculation; and \\textsl{MarketShare} is defined as firm revenue relative to total industry revenue. The dependent variable is \\textsl{Institution}, defined as the ratio of shares held by institutional investors to total shares at year-end. The main explanatory variable is \\textsl{PTC} (a dummy variable that equals one if the city where the firm is located has established a patent trading center in or before year $t$, and zero otherwise). The set of control variables is consistent with that of the baseline regression. All regressions include firm, year, and city fixed effects. The robust $t$-statistics clustered by the firm are reported in parentheses. The last row reports the empirical $p$-value for the difference in the coefficients of \\textsl{PTC} between the high and low groups, computed via a bootstrap Fisher permutation test with 500 replications. Detailed variable definitions are presented in Table \\ref{definition}. ***, **, and * denote the significance at the 1\\%, 5\\%, and 10\\% levels, respectively.\\par}}\n\\bigskip\n\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\textwidth}{lYYYYYYYY@{}}\n\\toprule\n\n                &\\multicolum