      "content": "# Edmans (2024) Rejection Guardrails — Quick Reference\n\nSource: Alex Edmans, \"Learnings From 1000 Rejections,\" Financial Management, 2025, 54:419-444.\nBased on 1,059 manuscripts handled as editor/managing editor of Review of Finance (2016-2022).\n\n## Category 1: CONTRIBUTION Problems\n\n### 1.1 Insufficiently Novel (Convex Combination)\n- If X→Z and Z→Y are known, showing X→Y adds little\n- Extending US results to another country without institutional reasons for different predictions\n- Extending from one type of investment to another (capex → R&D)\n- Extending peer effects from one corporate policy to another\n- TEST: \"Would a reader Bayesian update much after reading this paper?\"\n\n### 1.2 Insufficiently Important\n- \"Just another determinant of Y\" added to a long list\n- \"Just another outcome of X\" added to a long list\n- A \"curiosum\" — interesting but not first-order\n- Documenting channels that don't change interpretation of results\n- TEST: \"Would a survey paper on Y mention your X?\"\n- TEST: \"Would a policymaker change behavior based on this finding?\"\n\n### 1.3 Doesn't Fit General-Interest Finance\n- Topic is really accounting, OB, or macro\n- Topic is too niche for general audience\n- Bibliography check: if most references are to field journals, submit to a field journal\n\n### 1.4 Insufficiently Generalizable\n- Single-event study trying to draw general conclusions\n- Narrow sample with broad claims\n\n### 1.5 One Side of Trade-off\n- Studying whether X creates value but only documenting benefits\n- Must address both costs and benefits\n\n### 1.6 Lacks Clear Hypotheses\n- No directional predictions\n- \"Kitchen-sink\" regressions without theory\n- Hypothesis about Y1 but studying Y2 (complements vs substitutes unclear)\n- \"It's an empirical question\" is a weak defense\n\n## Category 2: EXECUTION Problems\n\n### 2.1 Instrumental Variables\n- Must explain IV in introduction, not bury it\n- Must justify BOTH relevance AND exclusion restriction\n- \"Finding valid instruments is very difficult\" — burden is on the author\n\n### 2.2 Measurement Issues\n- Proxy doesn't precisely measure the phenomenon\n- Quantity vs quality confusion (e.g., counting votes ≠ governance quality)\n\n### 2.3 Robustness\n- Results must survive alternative measures, controls, specifications\n- Small samples may be driven by outliers\n\n### 2.4 Alternative Explanations\n- Beyond reverse causality and omitted variables\n- Even causal results can have multiple interpretations\n- Showing X causes Y doesn't mean X is good/bad without interpretation\n\n## Category 3: EXPOSITION Problems\n\n### 3.1 Overclaiming\n- Claiming causation from correlation\n- Claiming generalizability from narrow sample\n- Title/abstract promising more than paper delivers\n\n### 3.2 Insufficient Motivation\n- Not explaining WHY the question matters\n- Not connecting to broader economic significance\n\n## The Golden Rules for Proposal Design\n\n1. **Change the reader's prior** — if a reader spends hours on your paper, they should learn something they didn't know or couldn't have guessed\n2. **Pass the survey paper test** — would this finding get its own paragraph in a future survey?\n3. **Every hypothesis needs a direction** — no fishing expeditions\n4. **Identification must be front and center** — explain your strategy in the introduction\n5. **Acknowledge limitations honestly** — editors respect transparency\n6. **Mechanisms matter when they change interpretation** — not all channels are equally interesting\n"
    },
