      "path": "paper-style/references/journal-profiles.md",
      "encoding": "utf8",
      "content": "# Journal House-Style Profiles\n\nTitle and section-structure conventions for common target journals. Each profile covers the same dimensions:\n\n- **Title**: phrasing, case, subtitle/colon tolerance\n- **Numbering**: section/subsection numbering scheme\n- **Heading case**: sentence case vs. title case\n- **Introduction**: numbered, unnumbered, or headingless\n- **Skeleton**: the canonical empirical-paper section sequence\n- **Literature review**: standalone section or folded into the introduction\n- **Hypotheses**: formally numbered statements vs. predictions in prose\n- **Section count**: typical range for the main body\n\nThese are *conventions extracted from recently published articles*, not submission-system requirements. When a paper's content genuinely doesn't fit the canonical skeleton, follow the journal's spirit (compactness, naming style, case) rather than forcing sections to exist.\n\n## Contents\n\n| Journal | Family |\n|---|---|\n| [Journal of Financial Economics (JFE)](#jfe) | Elsevier finance |\n| [Journal of Finance (JF)](#jf) | Wiley finance |\n| [Review of Financial Studies (RFS)](#rfs) | OUP finance |\n| [JFQA](#jfqa) | Cambridge finance |\n| [Management Science](#management-science) | INFORMS |\n| [American Economic Review (AER)](#aer) | AEA |\n| [Quarterly Journal of Economics (QJE)](#qje) | OUP economics |\n| [RAND Journal of Economics](#rand) | Wiley economics |\n| [Journal of International Economics (JIE)](#jie) | Elsevier economics |\n| [Research Policy](#research-policy) | Elsevier innovation |\n| [JCF / JBF](#jcf-jbf) | Elsevier finance |\n| [Accounting: JAR / TAR / JAE](#accounting) | — |\n| [Unknown journals: family mapping](#family-mapping) | — |\n\n---\n\n## JFE\n\n**Journal of Financial Economics** — Elsevier finance family.\n\n- **Title**: short, declarative noun phrase in sentence-grade concision; \"X and Y: Evidence from Z\" subtitles acceptable; question titles rare. Creative short titles are fine if informative.\n- **Numbering**: Arabic — `1.`, `2.`; subsections `2.1`, `2.1.1`.\n- **Heading case**: **sentence case** (\"2. Data and sample construction\"). Keep proper nouns/acronyms capitalized.\n- **Introduction**: numbered, \"1. Introduction\".\n- **Skeleton**:\n  1. Introduction (contribution + literature positioning folded in)\n  2. Institutional background and/or hypotheses *(optional, short)*\n  3. Data and sample *(or \"Data and summary statistics\")*\n  4. Empirical design and main results *(may carry a descriptive name, e.g., \"4. Ratings and fund flows\")*\n  5. Mechanisms / channels\n  6. Robustness and additional analyses *(commonly \"Additional analyses\")*\n  7. Conclusion\n- **Literature review**: never standalone; folded into the introduction.\n- **Hypotheses**: predictions in prose; formally numbered hypotheses are atypical — fold a \"Hypothesis Development\" section into a short background section or the introduction.\n- **Section count**: 6–8. Ten or more narrative sections is a clear restructuring signal: merge dynamics/welfare/cross-validation material into \"Additional analyses\".\n- **Avoid**: narrative section names (\"How Issuers Respond to a Downgrade\"), three-concept headings (\"Robustness, the Platform Era, and Placebo Tests\"), mechanism subsections styled \"Channel 1 -- ...\" (use plain descriptive subsection names).\n\n## JF\n\n**Journal of Finance** — Wiley.\n\n- **Title**: Title Case, moderately short; occasional question titles.\n- **Numbering**: Roman numerals — `I.`, `II.`; subsections lettered `A.`, `B.`; sub-subsections `A.1`.\n- **Heading case**: **title case** (\"II. Data and Methodology\").\n- **Introduction**: typically **headingless and unnumbered** — body text begins right after the abstract; the first numbered section is \"I. ...\" (often the data or framework section). Some papers do number \"I. Introduction\"; prefer the headingless convention when restructuring unless the user objects.\n- **Skeleton**: [unnumbered introduction] → I. Hypotheses/Framework or Data → II. Empirical Methodology → III. Main Results → IV. Mechanisms/Additional Results → V. Conclusion.\n- **Literature review**: folded into the introduction.\n- **Hypotheses**: prose predictions; light formal numbering tolerated in a framework section.\n- **Section count**: 5–7 Roman sections.\n- **Note**: robustness material often lives in an **Internet Appendix**; keep the main body lean.\n\n## RFS\n\n**Review of Financial Studies** — OUP.\n\n- **Title**: Title Case; question titles (\"Do X ...?\") are relatively common and acceptable.\n- **Numbering**: Arabic — `1.`, `2.`; subsections `2.1`.\n- **Heading case**: **title case** (\"2. Data and Variable Construction\").\n- **Introduction**: numbered, \"1. Introduction\".\n- **Skeleton**: similar to JFE — 1. Introduction; 2. (Background/Hypotheses); 3. Data; 4. Main results; 5. Mechanisms; 6. Robustness; 7. Conclusion.\n- **Literature review**: folded into the introduction.\n- **Hypotheses**: prose predictions; a short \"Testable implications\" subsection is acceptable.\n- **Section count**: 6–8.\n\n## JFQA\n\n**Journal of Financial and Quantitative Analysis** — Cambridge.\n\n- **Title**: Title Case, concise.\n- **Numbering**: Roman numerals `I.`, `II.`; subsections `A.`, `B.` (JF-like).\n- **Heading case**: title case.\n- **Introduction**: numbered \"I. Introduction\".\n- **Skeleton/lit review/hypotheses/count**: as JF, but the introduction carries a heading.\n\n## Management Science\n\nINFORMS.\n\n- **Title**: declarative or question; colon subtitles common; somewhat longer titles tolerated than JFE/JF.\n- **Numbering**: Arabic — `1.`, `2.`; subsections `2.1`.\n- **Heading case**: **title case** (\"2. Theory and Hypotheses\").\n- **Introduction**: numbered, \"1. Introduction\".\n- **Skeleton**:\n  1. Introduction\n  2. Related Literature *and/or* Theory and Hypotheses *(standalone — this is the big difference from finance journals)*\n  3. Institutional Setting *(if relevant)*\n  4. Data and Empirical Strategy\n  5. Results\n  6. Mechanisms / Additional Analyses\n  7. Discussion and Conclusion *(managerial/policy implications welcome)*\n- **Literature review**: standalone section is standard and expected.\n- **Hypotheses**: **formally stated and numbered** — \"Hypothesis 1 (H1). ...\" — developed in the theory section and referenced in results.\n- **Section count**: 6–8.\n\n## AER\n\n**American Economic Review** — AEA family (also AEJ: Applied, AEJ: Policy, etc.).\n\n- **Title**: short, declarative; restrained subtitles.\n- **Numbering**: Roman numerals `I.`, `II.`; subsections `A.`, `B.`.\n- **Heading case**: title case (\"II. Theoretical Framework\").\n- **Introduction**: **headingless and unnumbered** — text begins directly; first heading is \"I. ...\".\n- **Skeleton**: [introduction] → I. Background/Model → II. Data → III. Empirical Strategy → IV. Results → V. Conclusion.\n- **Literature review**: folded into the introduction.\n- **Hypotheses**: prose; model-derived predictions.\n- **Section count**: 5–7.\n\n## QJE\n\n**Quarterly Journal of Economics** — OUP.\n\n- **Title**: short, Title Case; QJE titles are famously punchy.\n- **Numbering**: Roman numerals; subsections `I.A`, `I.B`.\n- **Heading case**: title case (the journal typesets headings in caps; manuscripts use title case).\n- **Introduction**: numbered, \"I. Introduction\".\n- **Skeleton**: I. Introduction → II. Background/Model → III. Data → IV. Empirical Strategy and Results → V. Mechanisms → VI. Conclusion.\n- **Literature review**: folded into the introduction.\n- **Section count**: 5–7.\n\n## RAND\n\n**RAND Journal of Economics** — Wiley.\n\n- **Title**: concise; question titles common.\n- **Numbering**: Arabic `1.`, `2.`; subsections often short bold side-heads.\n- **Heading case**: **sentence case** (\"2. The model\").\n- **Introduction**: numbered \"1. Introduction\".\n- **Skeleton**: theory-forward — 1. Introduction; 2. Institutional background; 3. Model *(or theoretical framework — if the paper has no model, a \"Conceptual framework\" section with testable predictions serves)*; 4. Data; 5. Empirical results; 6. Conclusion.\n- **Literature review**: folded into the introduction.\n- **Hypotheses**: model-derived predictions in prose.\n- **Section count**: 5–7.\n- **Caution**: if the paper lacks any theoretical framing, flag it to the user — RAND readers expect one; do not fabricate it.\n\n## JIE\n\n**Journal of International Economics** — Elsevier.\n\n- **Title**: sentence-style concision; \"Evidence from\" subtitles common.\n- **Numbering**: Arabic; subsections `2.1`.\n- **Heading case**: **sentence case**.\n- **Introduction**: numbered \"1. Introduction\".\n- **Skeleton**: 1. Introduction; 2. (Stylized facts / Measurement — common in JIE); 3. (Model — optional); 4. Data; 5. Empirical results; 6. Robustness; 7. Conclusion.\n- **Literature review**: folded into the introduction.\n- **Section count**: 6–8.\n\n## Research Policy\n\nElsevier innovation/management.\n\n- **Title**: longer descriptive titles and colon subtitles are normal; questions acceptable.\n- **Numbering**: Arabic; subsections `2.1`.\n- **Heading case**: **sentence case** (\"2. Literature review and hypotheses\").\n- **Introduction**: numbered \"1. Introduction\".\n- **Skeleton**:\n  1. Introduction\n  2. Literature review and hypotheses *(standalone, with numbered H1/H2...)*\n  3. Institutional background *(for policy/China papers)*\n  4. Data and methods\n  5. Results\n  6. Discussion *(theoretical contributions, policy implications, limitations — often subsections)*\n  7. Conclusion *(or \"6. Discussion and conclusions\" combined)*\n- **Literature review**: standalone and expected.\n- **Hypotheses**: formally numbered (H1, H2, ...).\n- **Section count**: 6–7.\n\n## JCF / JBF\n\n**Journal of Corporate Finance / Journal of Banking & Finance** — Elsevier.\n\n- Same mechanics as JFE: Arabic numbering, **sentence case**, numbered \"1. Introduction\".\n- **Difference from JFE**: a short standalone \"2. Literature and hypothesis development\" section is tolerated and common; formally numbered hypotheses (H1/H2) acceptable.\n- **Skeleton**: 1. Introduction; 2. Literature and hypotheses; 3. Data and methodology; 4. Empirical results; 5. Robustness/additional tests; 6. Conclusion.\n- **Section count**: 6–7.\n\n## Accounting\n\n**JAR / TAR / JAE.**\n\n- **Numbering**: Arabic. **Heading case**: JAE (Elsevier) sentence case; JAR and TAR title case (TAR typesets small caps).\n- **Introduction**: numbered \"1. Introduction\" (JAR often Roman \"1.\" styling; keep Arabic in manuscript).\n- **Skeleton**: 1. Introduction; 2. Background and hypothesis development *(standalone — standard in accounting)*; 3. Research design and data; 4. Results; 5. Additional analyses; 6. Conclusion.\n- **Hypotheses**: formally numbered (H1, H2), often stated in italics.\n- **Section count**: 6–7.\n\n## Family Mapping\n\nFor a journal not profiled above, map by publisher/field and confirm with the user:\n\n| If the journal is... | Use profile | Adjust |\n|---|---|---|\n| Elsevier finance/economics (e.g., JFI, JEBO, JEDC, China Economic Review, PBFJ) | JFE | lit-review tolerance is higher outside the top 3 |\n| Wiley/OUP/Chicago top-5 economics (JPE, ReStud, Econometrica) | AER | Econometrica: numbered \"1. Introduction\", heavier model sections |\n| AEA journals (AEJ family) | AER | — |\n| INFORMS (Org Science, M&SOM) | Management Science | — |\n| Management/strategy (SMJ, AMJ, JMS) | Management Science | hypothesis development even more central |\n| Field finance (RF, FM, JFS) | RFS | — |\n| Innovation/policy (JPIM, Technovation, JTT) | Research Policy | — |\n\nWhen even the family is unclear, fetch the latest issue's table of contents and skim 2–3 recent articles for: title phrasing, numbering scheme, heading case, lit-review placement, and section count. Summarize the derived profile to the user before applying it.\n"
    },
